Mesa (KosmicKrisp): 16-byte aligned threadgroup memory

What: nir_to_msl declares a compute shader's shared memory as
'alignas(16) threadgroup char shared_data[N]' instead of a plain char array.
Why: KosmicKrisp reads and writes shared memory through casts such as
'*(threadgroup uint*)&shared_data[offset]'. A plain char array is only 1-byte
aligned, and the MSL compiler then makes these accesses far slower. WebGPU
f32 matmul (src/bench/pages/webgpu-matmul.html: 2048x2048, two 16x64
workgroup tiles) in a VM on a MacBook Air M2, macOS 27.0.1, Chromium's WebGPU
launcher: 254-258 GFLOPS before, 747-770 after, MoltenVK 892-896 in the same
VM (2 starts per driver, 3 runs each). computeBoids (60 fps) and vkpeak
(fp32 2,587) did not change. Before this, a Mac mini M4 gave KosmicKrisp
1,135 against MoltenVK 1,630 on the same page. Only the array's own alignment
changes; the offsets and the access types stay as they were.
Upstream: not sent yet (a draft is in the kk-webgpu track notes).
Tests: Vulkan CTS 1.4.7.0 on KosmicKrisp before and after (all compute
pipeline cases but cooperative_matrix, all memory_model, all spirv_assembly
compute, glsl loops/arrays/indexing, graphicsfuzz, samples of subgroups and
tessellation/geometry): 50,788 cases, the same result for every case. WebGPU
matmul, computeBoids and vkpeak in a VM (numbers above).
--- a/src/kosmickrisp/compiler/nir_to_msl.c
+++ b/src/kosmickrisp/compiler/nir_to_msl.c
@@ -127,7 +127,10 @@
 emit_local_vars(struct nir_to_msl_ctx *ctx, nir_shader *shader)
 {
    if (shader->info.shared_size) {
-      P_IND(ctx, "threadgroup char shared_data[%d];\n",
+      /* Shared memory is accessed through casts to 4- and 16-byte types. A
+       * plain char array is only 1-byte aligned, and the MSL compiler then
+       * emits much slower accesses (3x slower tiled matmul on M2). */
+      P_IND(ctx, "alignas(16) threadgroup char shared_data[%d];\n",
             shader->info.shared_size);
    }
    if (shader->scratch_size) {
